  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive breakdown of the meaning of the word **INDOLENT**:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcd6 Definition\n\n**Indolent** is an **adjective** meaning **slow or lazy**. More specifically, it means being **averse to activity, effort, or movement \u2014 habitually lazy**.\n\nAn indolent person is slow and lazy \u2014 not the type of person you'd want running your corporation or competing with you in a relay race.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udfe5 Medical Use\n\nDoctors also use the word **indolent** to describe **medical conditions that are slow to progress**. If you're diagnosed with an illness, you'd prefer an indolent one over one that spreads quickly.\n\nIn pathology, it can also mean **causing little pain**, and especially of a painless ulcer, being **slow to heal**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd24 Synonyms\n\nSynonyms include: **lazy, slack, idle, slow, sluggish, inactive, inert, languid, lethargic, listless, lackadaisical, torpid, slothful**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf31 Word Origin (Etymology)\n\nThe word comes from **Latin** *indol\u0113ns*, meaning **\"not feeling pain\"**, from *in-* (not) + *dol\u0113ns*, from *dol\u0113re* meaning **\"to grieve\" or \"cause distress.\"**\n\nThe main modern sense of **\"laziness, love of ease\"** dates to around **1710**, perhaps reflecting the notion of **avoiding trouble**.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udcac Example Sentence\n\n*\"The indolent student spent the entire day on the couch instead of studying for his exams.\"*",
